WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:04.710
Film Courage: What are the biggest mistakes
you see new screenwriters make for a screenplay?

00:00:04.710 --> 00:00:09.730
Dr. Ken Atchity, producer/author: What do
you mean?

00:00:09.730 --> 00:00:13.490
Film Courage: Structurally, character, dialogue,
length?

00:00:13.490 --> 00:00:18.189
Dr. Atchity: Um…because the biggest mistakes
are usually their personalities.

00:00:18.189 --> 00:00:21.110
Film Courage: Of the actual writer?

00:00:21.110 --> 00:00:22.110
Dr. Atchity: Yeah.

00:00:22.110 --> 00:00:23.189
Film Courage: Oh, okay…

00:00:23.189 --> 00:00:32.200
Dr. Atchity: But that aside I think the biggest
mistake is over-explaining and not knowing

00:00:32.200 --> 00:00:37.970
how to tunnel the background of the story
into the story instead of laying it out.

00:00:37.970 --> 00:00:43.630
Like we just developed a brilliant screenplay
the last several years in which the opening

00:00:43.630 --> 00:00:52.410
conversation just is not convincing because
there is no other explanations for the words

00:00:52.410 --> 00:00:57.090
in the conversation other than explaining
to the audience than what is going on.

00:00:57.090 --> 00:01:02.110
And that’s a hard-to-avoid mistake because
you know you’ve got to explain what’s

00:01:02.110 --> 00:01:03.420
going on.

00:01:03.420 --> 00:01:07.920
But if you do it overtly the audience is not
going to believe the dialogue.

00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:13.450
They are not going to be able to suspend their
disbelief.

00:01:13.450 --> 00:01:23.259
So you have to sneak these things in the story
at times when they’re needed and not too-much-too-soon.

00:01:23.259 --> 00:01:28.840
One of the common mistakes, the root mistake
beneath that mistake is thinking that the

00:01:28.840 --> 00:01:32.109
audience is not as smart as they are.

00:01:32.109 --> 00:01:35.829
It’s looking down on your audience.

00:01:35.829 --> 00:01:40.259
Because respecting the audience is essential
to good storytelling.

00:01:40.259 --> 00:01:43.719
You have to believe in the audience.

00:01:43.719 --> 00:01:49.429
Imagine sitting on the country porch and telling
a story with no audience, right?

00:01:49.429 --> 00:01:55.200
I find that very hard to imagine because storytelling
is about audiences.

00:01:55.200 --> 00:02:00.939
You can immediately start telling the story
if even one person shows up.

00:02:00.939 --> 00:02:04.119
But sitting there and telling it by yourself?

00:02:04.119 --> 00:02:06.189
That isn’t what a story is.

00:02:06.189 --> 00:02:11.790
So if one person shows up and it’s Jackie,
you are going to tell it differently than

00:02:11.790 --> 00:02:14.250
if Sally showed up first.

00:02:14.250 --> 00:02:17.040
And if Sally and Jackie are there together
you are going to tell it differently then

00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:19.049
either one of them by themselves.

00:02:19.049 --> 00:02:22.239
That’s just the nature of communication.

00:02:22.239 --> 00:02:29.010
So not respecting the audience or not realizing
that it’s all about the audience is probably

00:02:29.010 --> 00:02:30.740
the biggest mistake.

00:02:30.740 --> 00:02:34.769
And it takes awhile to get out of that mistake
because the only way you can get out it is

00:02:34.769 --> 00:02:39.390
through constant feedback that tells you it’s
not necessary.

00:02:39.390 --> 00:02:43.409
Because someone once told me an editor is
a person who tells an editor when to stop

00:02:43.409 --> 00:02:47.250
writing and I thought there was a lot of truth
to that.

00:02:47.250 --> 00:02:52.420
Because how is the writer going to know unless
somebody says “You don’t need that.”

00:02:52.420 --> 00:02:59.870
And I think that is often the case with screenwriters
is they put a lot of things in that they don’t

00:02:59.870 --> 00:03:00.870
need.

00:03:00.870 --> 00:03:03.150
We need much less then we think we do.

00:03:03.150 --> 00:03:08.800
We don’t need a scene to come to an end,
it’s okay to cut out of it.

00:03:08.800 --> 00:03:14.760
I mean writers are constantly have a great
line at the end of the scene but then they

00:03:14.760 --> 00:03:21.150
add a couple more lines (insecurity lines
I call them) so we instantly just cross them

00:03:21.150 --> 00:03:24.500
out because we already just had a great line,
that’s the end of the scene.

00:03:24.500 --> 00:03:29.590
And yes, everything hasn’t been quite ramped
up and that adds energy and when you cut that

00:03:29.590 --> 00:03:32.349
you’re pouring energy into the story.

00:03:32.349 --> 00:03:36.830
When you leave it there you are sucking energy
out of the story because the audience is like

00:03:36.830 --> 00:03:42.120
bored through the last two lines and is like
“Oh, I’m out of it.

00:03:42.120 --> 00:03:45.000
You had me at hello” kind of thing.

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:49.810
And I think that’s probably the most common
mistake is overwriting, just overwriting.

00:03:49.810 --> 00:03:53.520
Film Courage: You said earlier about personality?

00:03:53.520 --> 00:03:58.420
Dr. Atchity: Well, screenwriters have to be
careful with their personalities.

00:03:58.420 --> 00:04:08.870
By nature they are very (overtly at least)
self-confident, aggressive people.

00:04:08.870 --> 00:04:15.189
Mostly that’s a mask for lack of confidence
which is a normal thing for a writer to have.

00:04:15.189 --> 00:04:20.239
We all have that no matter what stage you
reach, you will always have a lack of self-confidence.

00:04:20.239 --> 00:04:24.340
But then the world is divided into those who
do it anyway.

00:04:24.340 --> 00:04:27.000
And those who let them stop that.

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:30.150
You know, every actor has that before he goes
on stage.

00:04:30.150 --> 00:04:37.690
Some of the greatest actors (Richard Chamberlin)
used to throw up before he went on stage.

00:04:37.690 --> 00:04:41.640
But he went on stage, I mean it was just part
of what he did.

00:04:41.640 --> 00:04:46.270
You get nervous before you start and that’s
not a bad thing, that’s a good thing.

00:04:46.270 --> 00:04:51.250
You know, if you weren’t nervous then you
are not doing something important.

00:04:51.250 --> 00:04:55.069
So if you are going into battle on the battlefield,
should you be afraid, yes!

00:04:55.069 --> 00:05:00.620
If you weren’t afraid, you’d be nuts.

00:05:00.620 --> 00:05:07.310
And so what happens in personality is that
sometimes people compensate in an over-aggressive

00:05:07.310 --> 00:05:13.610
way and it’s an automatic turn off because
one of the several things about succeeding

00:05:13.610 --> 00:05:18.000
in Hollywood is don’t get on everyone’s
life is too short list.

00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:24.310
And it’s really true and sometimes you get
on it immediately and you’ll never get off

00:05:24.310 --> 00:05:27.410
of it.

00:05:27.410 --> 00:05:32.960
And you have to realize that you need to act
with the people in the business the way you

00:05:32.960 --> 00:05:38.680
would act with true friends who will tell
you you’re an a**hole if you’re being

00:05:38.680 --> 00:05:39.680
an a**hole.

00:05:39.680 --> 00:05:43.750
And Hollywood won’t, they’ll just say
“Thank you so much for coming.

00:05:43.750 --> 00:05:45.520
We’ll be in touch with you.”

00:05:45.520 --> 00:05:47.020
And that will be the end of it.

00:05:47.020 --> 00:05:50.970
But they’ll go “A**hole as soon as he’s
out of the room.”

00:05:50.970 --> 00:05:56.569
And demanding, it’s partly demanding because
there is a kind of petulance that sets in

00:05:56.569 --> 00:06:03.620
the longer a writer has worked without reward
and acclimation and compensation.

00:06:03.620 --> 00:06:06.300
And it’s almost like “I deserve this.

00:06:06.300 --> 00:06:08.229
You are going to give it to me now.”

00:06:08.229 --> 00:06:12.169
You know, I had an actress at dinner one night
and in the middle of dinner she said (you

00:06:12.169 --> 00:06:17.190
know a lot of other people around) and she
said “I demand attention now!”

00:06:17.190 --> 00:06:22.630
And it was really funny because the men thought
that was cute and the women did not?

00:06:22.630 --> 00:06:23.630
She was not re-invited.

00:06:23.630 --> 00:06:24.630
Film Courage: On no…

00:06:24.630 --> 00:06:29.729
Dr. Atchity: No…the women definitely did
not like that petulance because men thought

00:06:29.729 --> 00:06:33.759
it was cute because she was se**y and so on.

00:06:33.759 --> 00:06:34.759
Film Courage: Sure, sure.

00:06:34.759 --> 00:06:39.759
Dr. Atchity: But in today’s world (especially
in Hollywood) where the stakes are so high,

00:06:39.759 --> 00:06:41.630
everybody wants to get in.

00:06:41.630 --> 00:06:48.040
Thousands of people are lined up for everything
single position you could ask for and they

00:06:48.040 --> 00:06:50.860
want people who are cool and professional.

00:06:50.860 --> 00:06:53.280
Not hot-headed and demanding.

00:06:53.280 --> 00:06:59.340
And when I see a writer is of the latter category
I just kind of run for the hills.

00:06:59.340 --> 00:07:06.010
And when I see a writer is not that way (is
modest and so on) I will continue talking

00:07:06.010 --> 00:07:10.750
to them for years even before I represent
him or work with him until I find something

00:07:10.750 --> 00:07:18.900
that I can work with because he’s so respectful
and not on my too-short-list.

00:07:18.900 --> 00:07:29.470
That’s a very rare thing but it’s important
when you think about it in any walk of life.

00:07:29.470 --> 00:07:33.979
The uncle who you can’t stand to see at
Thanksgiving is not the one that you want

00:07:33.979 --> 00:07:38.379
to be representing or making a movie with.

00:07:38.379 --> 00:07:43.690
Film Courage: Is that when someone is too
self-aggrandizing, like “This is the greatest

00:07:43.690 --> 00:07:44.690
thing!”

00:07:44.690 --> 00:07:47.030
And they won’t take no for an answer and
it just becomes “Please…”

00:07:47.030 --> 00:07:50.770
Dr. Atchity: Yeah, that’s part of it because
don’t tell us how great it is, just tell

00:07:50.770 --> 00:07:54.840
us the story, get out of the way of the story.

00:07:54.840 --> 00:07:59.280
We don’t want to hear this is your first
pitch, we don’t want to hear you haven’t

00:07:59.280 --> 00:08:00.490
done this much.

00:08:00.490 --> 00:08:04.570
We don’t want to hear that you practiced
this and you know exactly what to say, we

00:08:04.570 --> 00:08:07.629
want to see if you’ve got the goods and
that is the story.

00:08:07.629 --> 00:08:11.581
Like Mark Twain said “Don’t tell us the
fat lady is going to sing, bring her out and

00:08:11.581 --> 00:08:12.581
let her sing.”

00:08:12.581 --> 00:08:17.240
You know, that’s what we want to see.

00:08:17.240 --> 00:08:23.590
In other words, don’t draw attention to
yourself because that is not what we’re

00:08:23.590 --> 00:08:24.590
interested in.

00:08:24.590 --> 00:08:26.599
We are interested in the story.

00:08:26.599 --> 00:08:30.992
And maybe we’ll get interested in you later
like after you’ve brought a couple of good

00:08:30.992 --> 00:08:36.130
stories in and then we go “By the way, where
are you from and what’s your story?”

00:08:36.130 --> 00:08:39.050
But we don’t want to deal with it at the
beginning.

00:08:39.050 --> 00:08:44.660
Because the truth is no matter who you are,
if your story is good, we love you because

00:08:44.660 --> 00:08:50.970
you told this story and that’s a great kind
of equalizer when you think about it, it’s

00:08:50.970 --> 00:08:54.329
a real world of ideas.

00:08:54.329 --> 00:08:58.890
But if you start telling us about yourself
and give us the chance to hate you before

00:08:58.890 --> 00:09:03.070
you even started telling the story, then how
stupid is that.

00:09:03.070 --> 00:09:10.700
And I have to say years ago I stopped taking
writers to pitches of their stories because

00:09:10.700 --> 00:09:14.800
they most of the time unsold the story after
they sold it.

00:09:14.800 --> 00:09:15.800
Film Courage: Oh no.

00:09:15.800 --> 00:09:19.410
Dr. Atchity: For a very simple reason, they
weren’t looking at the buyer.

00:09:19.410 --> 00:09:20.890
They were in a coma.

00:09:20.890 --> 00:09:25.120
They were telling the story and they weren’t
watching the buyers eyes which is all that

00:09:25.120 --> 00:09:27.360
matters in a pitch.

00:09:27.360 --> 00:09:30.769
Like I can see immediately if you’re bored.

00:09:30.769 --> 00:09:37.440
And if I’m anybody at all, I’ll switch
to another story to pitch you.

00:09:37.440 --> 00:09:43.519
Because the buyer is thinking “Do I like
this story, can I do something with it?”

00:09:43.519 --> 00:09:47.400
But sometimes he’ll just hear one word,
dragon and that will be it “Click” my

00:09:47.400 --> 00:09:52.970
eyes go “Let’s see, I’ve got a 3:15
and a 3:30” like my brain is thinking of

00:09:52.970 --> 00:09:58.100
other things because I know I can’t acquire
a dragon story, so why am I wasting this person’s

00:09:58.100 --> 00:09:59.100
time?

00:09:59.100 --> 00:10:03.680
Well the person is not even listening and
looking at me so he goes on talking.

00:10:03.680 --> 00:10:05.149
But the other thing happens, too.

00:10:05.149 --> 00:10:09.220
He’s already said enough to sell you, you’re
totally…this is what you’re looking for,

00:10:09.220 --> 00:10:12.570
I can see it in your eyes, but you keep talking.

00:10:12.570 --> 00:10:14.480
So after a while now, the buyer is looking
at him, instead of listening to the story

00:10:14.480 --> 00:10:19.480
he’s looking at him instead of listening
to the story he’s looking to him going “Why

00:10:19.480 --> 00:10:20.889
is this guy still talking?

00:10:20.889 --> 00:10:21.889
I love this!

00:10:21.889 --> 00:10:22.990
I want to ask him questions.”

00:10:22.990 --> 00:10:24.490
You know what I mean?

00:10:24.490 --> 00:10:30.970
So that’s an example of a writer forgetting
about the most important part of his story

00:10:30.970 --> 00:10:32.940
which is his audience.

00:10:32.940 --> 00:10:38.399
His audience, the reader, his audience is
the most important part of the story and when

00:10:38.399 --> 00:10:46.240
you forget it, they know (the reader knows)
that you’ve forgotten it and then they’re

00:10:46.240 --> 00:10:48.740
tuned out, they close the book.

